1795385866848454	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-2ss	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1lv"}	blake3:dxPBbliVA3Mr1vzwvXV3Q-DjAlmsvJl6erJsDRJT0vw
1795385931415492	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2el	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEverything a read says about a lead comes from one pure function over the on-chain records that name it. There is no stored `verified` flag and no promotion transaction. It works in three steps: judge each record on its own (`broken`, `stale`, `stands`), pick the status record S (the latest true `verify` record) and the deciding record D, and read the answer off them. Records are ordered by chain sequence, never by a timestamp.\n\n# Do\n`Status.compute(lead, snapshot, audience) -> status, applicability, S, D`, exactly as plan §5.1:\n- status: `integrity_failure`, `lead` (with sub-state and last outcome), `refuted`, `needs_review`, `disputed`, `conditional`, `verified`. Integrity checks come first.\n- the rule that applies to S is the admitted rule in force at its run, or the first rule admitted for that resource afterwards. So a `conditional` finding becomes `verified` with no new run.\n- applicability from D: `as_observed`, `still_violated`, `fixed_within_tested_scope`, `unestablished`.\n- re-evaluate the oracle over retained originals; a mismatch or a missing original is `broken`.\n- the audience rule: for `third_party`, only records acquired in isolated custody qualify. M1 needs the function to take the parameter; the third-party cases are exercised in M2.5.\n- the retraction branch exists only where the shipped Investigation offers the query.\nA memo by record, oracle and rule digests is a cache and never an input.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Deterministic examples identify S, D, status and applicability. The sibling M1.6.6 bone owns the generated-history regression suite.\n- [ ] F-04: one saved true observation gives `verified`, `conditional`, `conditional`, `refuted` under the four policy situations.\n- [ ] F-21 revoked oracle version is `needs_review`. F-22: class or slot change is `needs_review`; text change changes nothing; rule admission gives `verified` with no new run.\n- [ ] Re-evaluation that detects a changed verdict or memo fails with INTEGRITY_FAILURE (F-23). A missing required original also fails integrity (F-24).\n- [ ] No table or file stores status or applicability.\n\nUse the latest qualifying true record for the audience. An invalid run cannot become D. A missing original for either S or D fails integrity, and a newer true record supersedes the previous baseline.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §5.6, §11.3; INV-02, INV-04 to INV-06, INV-50; F-04, F-21 to F-24.\n"}	blake3:lNW4kBKrtFhNPNvlYOWRaznh94oG1_0mruBGKxVPlp8
1795385931654416	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2s7	{"field":"description","value":"Generate small offline histories of immutable records, policy changes and evidence availability. Check the public status calculation against the expected record identities and outcomes in plan §5.1. Keep the failing seed and minimized history.\n\nAcceptance\n- Ordering follows chain sequence even when timestamps disagree. Inserting an invalid run after a true record leaves S and D in place. Editing only the lead text preserves the result. A conflicting cached verdict fails integrity on re-evaluation (F-23).\n- Check both S and D for missing originals. A new true record supersedes an older retest. A later deployment change or permissive replacement rule limits present applicability while preserving history (F-22, F-43, F-52, F-53).\n- The workspace and third-party projections of the same mixed-custody history reproduce F-50. Synthetic retest records exercise the pure function in M1. The packaged-report check belongs to M2.5.\n\nRun the properties in the ordinary test suite. See plan §13, INV-02, INV-04, INV-50 and INV-51."}	blake3:PqVLXqZsD3U8zpaCihhfFL0i8QAiCgwuku9-hP9kBos
1795385931880983	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2s7	{"field":"description","value":"Generate small offline histories of immutable records, policy changes and evidence availability. Check the public status calculation against the expected record identities and outcomes in plan §5.1. Keep the failing seed and minimized history.\n\nAcceptance\n- Ordering follows chain sequence even when timestamps disagree. Inserting an invalid run after a true record leaves S and D in place. Editing only the lead text preserves the result. A conflicting stored verdict or memo fails integrity on re-evaluation (F-23).\n- Check both S and D for missing originals. A new true record supersedes an older retest. A later deployment change or permissive replacement rule limits present applicability while preserving history (F-22, F-43, F-52, F-53).\n- The workspace and third-party projections of the same mixed-custody history reproduce F-50. Synthetic retest records exercise the pure function in M1. The packaged-report check belongs to M2.5.\n\nRun the properties in the ordinary test suite. See plan §13, INV-02, INV-04, INV-50 and INV-51."}	blake3:3KVi29fo1aZ-Wnrnoq9RBgGz0miV7G7NiKQjtvuqtZk
1795385932195279	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-1ia	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-1tz"}	blake3:vdLeRhjtl0J0550eR0oH18Xlc-pXhCss779N_HE528k
1795385932416105	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2og	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe conformance run itself. Waits for Investigation to ship its recording API (W07).\n\n# Do\nWire the adapter slot to Investigation's recording crate, or to its JSON protocol if the crate is not the public route. Run every fixture. Record the Investigation version.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every contract fixture passes against a named Investigation version through its public interface.\n- [ ] Each gap is filed as a named package in Investigation's tracker.\n- [ ] A documented blocker does not close this bone.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §9.2, §12.1 (\"Prerequisite\").\n\nUse the passing conformance record to finish the evidence-contract ADR required by plan §12.3. Name the public API and version, the minimum supported slice, capabilities actually exercised, and what would require requalification. M1.4 consumes this record.\n"}	blake3:u6lagwh2MU8roZhBU861HAgpyeXxizGmxSiYPwONpZ0
1795385932711332	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-r3b	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`show ID [--depth title|summary|full]` works for leads, findings, runs, records and instrument flow IDs. `list [leads|findings|runs]` is the bounded listing the entry screen's omission counts point to.\n\n# Do\n- Implement both as short read transactions.\n- Target content in any output is delimited as untrusted and size-bounded. This is hygiene, not a boundary. Raw evidence is opt-in.\n- A network-sink test over the whole read-only surface: status, help, `show`, `list`, `next`, `--plan`, `doctor` (INV-15).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] `show` and `list` tests on a seeded store.\n- [ ] The network-sink test fails if any read-only command opens a connection to the target.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.3, §8.4; INV-15, INV-22.\n\nImplement the read-only doctor view in this slice. On a seeded store it displays each recorded diagnostic and its age, identifies missing results, and leaves both stores untouched (plan §9.4). Run the network-sink assertion against the commands shipped at this point, and extend it as each later read command lands.\n"}	blake3:lb0KIZ1cbTZdtrGd4huL75L8KYBMTk1OWTrjehKkuDk
1795385933770626	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-1o3	{"description":"Implement the index-rebuild action of plan §9.4. On a copy of a workspace with a damaged index and intact chain, rebuild the derived index and compare record IDs, heads and history with the known-good copy.\n\nAcceptance\n- A repeat rebuild gives the same index. An interrupted rebuild leaves a consistent visible index or can be retried from the same chain.\n- A broken chain or missing original remains visible as an integrity failure. Preserve immutable evidence, unknown effects and pending work. Run reconciliation is covered by M1.7.\n- Document the distinction between read-only diagnosis and this local repair, with an example that uses a copy of the workspace.","kind":"task","labels":["m2","surface","test"],"parent":"bn-1hy","size":"s","title":"M2.9.3 Rebuild the local record index from its chain"}	blake3:1xfMdgxj4JUUQg3CeRf8EFHPWQNregv_f8SGY0cIuH0
