1795387075531733	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.link	bn-38z	{"link_type":"blocks","target":"bn-9dy"}	blake3:GTgYFXlXSOn2afomHNt5NMySF4h0LSOr0LRJYJa_ywk
1795387075571042	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-3og	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nFirst code in the repository. The plan's layout (§11.1) is a Rust workspace with `crates/`, `packs/`, `fixtures/` and `evals/`. The fixture target is a small web application the oracles run against.\n\n# Do\n- Create the workspace and a `fixtures/target/` crate that builds one self-contained binary listening on a configurable address.\n- Accounts: alice and bob in different tenants, an admin, and a second account of each user role for the verifier (alice-verify, bob-verify). Separate verifier principals are how target isolation is achieved (plan §3.4), so the fixture needs four test accounts, not two.\n- Cookie-session login and logout, and an identity route `GET /app/api/me` whose JSON has a stable subject at `/id`.\n- `POST /app/_reset` to return to the seeded state, reachable only with an administration secret.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] `cargo build` produces the fixture binary; `cargo test` covers login, logout and the identity route for every account.\n- [ ] Two different session labels for one account return the same subject (needed for fixture F-01).\n- [ ] Reset restores the seeded state.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §4.6, §11.1.\n\nAlso accept: one documented command, run from the repository root, runs every test in the repository. Later bones add their tests to it, and M1.12 audits what it covers.\n"}	blake3:444Gj3F6eQWixZk8IMQkrsJ3ds3mJzlCz7qvM8Mrqzk
1795387075605149	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-73w	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe M1 bones were written from plan revision 5, before any measurement. Use revision 5 and any owner-approved freeze note as the baseline.\n\n# Do\n- Reconcile notes/plan.md with the M0.1 inventory, the M0.4.5 ADR and the frozen contracts. Apply the owner's decision on the freeze note and record any resulting revision.\n- Go through every M1 bone: close what the M0 decision cut, fix what the contracts changed, split anything that is now clearly too large.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every open M1 bone matches the frozen contracts.\n- [ ] Bones cut by the M0 decision are closed with a comment that names the ADR.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §12.1, §12.3.\n\n# Also\n- If the M0 decision re-scoped M1, or the inventory changed a design fact, update the README's status and roadmap to match.\n- If the owner changed the Investigation dependency in M0.6.3, carry the decision into M1.4 and the M1 bones that read evidence.\n"}	blake3:l4KkpAj84pxCld16PcZZH_u3j7AlEi8sS-dWK-UqXmU
1795387075636104	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-tnk	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nA claim template belongs to a pack and is compiled into the verifier. Agents do not author controls. Slot values from a lead are untrusted. The slots are `owner`, `other`, `read`, `plant` and `remove`. A lead that names a declared property takes `read`, `plant` and `remove` from it and supplies the principal pair.\n\n`plant` and `remove` run with the owner's credentials. A plant request written by the agent can create the object already shared with `other`, and then every control passes on a false claim.\n\n# Do\n- `Templates.resolve(lead, engagement)` returns an experiment or the missing slots. The record says who supplied each slot.\n- `--slot` cannot override a slot the property fixes (`SLOT_CONFLICT`).\n- A `read` request supplied by a lead must use GET or HEAD, or the run is refused with `METHOD_REFUSED` and nothing is sent (INV-37).\n- An experiment whose owner-side requests came from a lead is marked so status can cap it at `conditional` (INV-36).\n- Freeze the experiment with a digest that binds template version, resolved slots, principal bindings, oracle version and limits, and not the canary.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Unit tests on `Templates.resolve`: a lead-supplied plant marks the experiment so that status caps it at `conditional`; a slot override is `SLOT_CONFLICT`; a DELETE or POST bound to the read slot is `METHOD_REFUSED`.\n- [ ] The experiment digest changes with each bound field and does not change with the canary.\n\nThe end-to-end halves are elsewhere: F-38 and F-39 run through the real verifier with a network sink in M1.5.4, and `--plan` announcing the cap is checked in M1.6.4. This bone needs no adapter, so it can start as soon as M1.1 is done (plan §12.3 lanes).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.2; INV-36, INV-37; F-38, F-39.\n"}	blake3:94ruRNLgwg8naRRHcsIsQ1XJqmEy6GgEAoBA1eI7cF4
1795387075668380	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-20u	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe oracle consumes the verifier's own typed observations for one round and returns `true`, `false`, `invalid` with reason codes, or `error`. Flows: identity probes I1, I2; plants P, P2 (two new objects, each with its own canaries); F0 anonymous read before the owner's; F1 owner read; F2 other-user read; F3 anonymous read; F4 other user reads the second object; identity probes I3, I4; cleanup C.\n\n# Do\n`Oracle.evaluate(observations) -> result`, pure:\n- `true`: the first object's canary is in F1 and absent from F4, the subjects are distinct and unchanged, and the canary is in F0, F2 or F3. Exposure is `unauthenticated` when it is in F0 or F3, otherwise `cross_principal`. Note `after_owner_read` when F0 lacks it and F3 has it.\n- `false`: setup valid and the canary absent from F0, F2 and F3.\n- `invalid`, never `false`: `SAME_SUBJECT`, `OBSERVER_INVALID`, `CANARY_NOT_SPECIFIC`, `SESSION_DRIFT`, `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n- Matching runs over the decoded body. Transfer and content codings are undone under a size and ratio limit and the decoder is named. A body over the limit is `EVIDENCE_INCOMPLETE`.\n- HTTP status is not an input (INV-08). The oracle never reads a policy rule.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Unit tests for each outcome and each reason code on saved observations.\n- [ ] The function's signature has no status-code field and no policy input.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.4, §6.5; INV-07, INV-08; F-42.\n\nThis function is pure and needs no adapter or evidence store, so it can start as soon as M1.1.1 is done (plan §12.3 lanes). M1.5.7 property-tests it over generated observations.\n"}	blake3:whK3H4xDR-bxMk02R361mGynvu1Z46G6enN-uh0P9u8
1795387075700241	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-36n	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nEach fixture pairs the right behavior with a tempting wrong conclusion. They are runnable cases under `fixtures/oracle/` and regression tests from here on.\n\n# Do\nImplement as end-to-end cases against the development fixture, through the real verifier and adapter:\nF-01 same subject under two labels; F-02 public object, with and without a rule; F-03 200 login page; F-05 403 carrying the canary; F-06 stale cache; F-07 session drift; F-08 canary echo; F-09 a canary or flow from an earlier run; F-10 agent-supplied flow IDs are ignored; F-11 missing or truncated flow; F-14 instrument or oracle crash; F-36 owner-only property with no authentication check; F-37 shared cache after the owner's read; F-38, F-39 (resolved in M1.5.1, run here end to end with a network sink); F-42 compressed and escaped canary, and an oversized body. F-11 and F-42 use the fixture modes of M1.5.6.\nF-04 needs the status function and is finished in M1.6.3.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every listed case runs in CI against the real fixture and passes with the result plan §14 requires.\n- [ ] No case passes by being skipped: a missing dependency fails the suite.\n\n# No claim\nPassing fixtures shows the oracle handles these cases. Whether each check is doing work is the mutation suite's job (M1.5.5).\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §14.\n"}	blake3:TUnao9-1Njl7HpN1fOh8jZlhGEvNYLpih0E7iKdNGmM
1795387075734900	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-23g	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`verify` and `retest` are the only porcelain commands that send target traffic. Each says what it will do, and its `--plan` form shows it first and sends nothing.\n\n# Do\n- `verify LEAD --plan`: the matched template, the resolved slots and who supplied each, any unfilled slot as a form, the flows and their count, the effects, the request ceiling from the dry run, the custody, target isolation and enforcement the run would have, and the highest status the result can reach with the policy rule as it stands. For a class with no template: `ORACLE_UNAVAILABLE`, the supported classes, and that the lead stays a lead.\n- `verify LEAD [--rounds N]`: the ten-step run. Two rounds by default; two true rounds is `reproduced`.\n- No command, file, import or API accepts a verdict, an oracle result or a record from a caller (INV-01).\n- An instrument's own confidence, verification state, fixed state or export is never an input (INV-48).\n- Only the operator admits a policy rule; a lead can propose one (INV-39).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] End to end on the fixture: lead, `--plan`, `verify`, and `show` reads `verified` with the full badge (INV-05).\n- [ ] F-48: promoting a Teapot lead, running `teapot findings repro --verify` until it says `fixed`, and exporting Teapot's report change no Scourge status and no applicability.\n- [ ] A catalog schema test shows no command has an input for a verdict.\n- [ ] `--plan` sends nothing (network sink).\n- [ ] F-38: for a lead whose plant came from the lead, `--plan` announces that the highest status the result can reach is `conditional`.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §2.3, §6.1, §8.2, §8.3; INV-01, INV-05, INV-15, INV-39, INV-48; F-48.\n"}	blake3:gOLqFiE5nldQthCI4M9BuZmGgKU--4fKDD_QuRNSLIA
1795387075776860	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2f7	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nOne immutable record per run, with the fields of plan §6.9. Each engagement has its own chain: a record contains the digest of the one before it. Publication is write-ahead so that recovery never puts a record on the chain that the verifier did not write.\n\n# Do\n- Before the verifier stores a record it persists a publication intent: engagement, run, record digest and the head it expects.\n- Then, in one transaction: link the record once for that run, check the head is still the expected one, advance it, mark the intent complete.\n- If the head has moved, nothing is linked, and the verifier rebuilds the record on the new head under a new intent.\n- `scourge run reconcile` finishes only a publication with a matching intent. An import cannot create an intent.\n- A record-shaped artifact with no intent is not on the chain and is ignored by status.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] F-20: an imported record-shaped artifact and a lookalike offered to `run reconcile` are never linked; one genuine pending publication reconciled twice at once yields one record and one head.\n- [ ] A broken chain is `INTEGRITY_FAILURE`.\n\n# No claim\nIn shared custody the chain detects accidents and casual edits. It does not stop a determined same-account forger, and reports say that.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §6.9, §9.3; INV-01, INV-28; F-20.\n\nThe record this bone publishes is the one M1.5.2.4 stored. Until this bone lands, a stored record is on no chain.\n"}	blake3:AZwARPdEp6zL6AH-9OiJzieJO03XF1TNPVlTdZW0WtQ
1795387075824119	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1aw	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nFollow the stack's conventions: `--format text|pretty|json`, a hidden `--json` alias, the `FORMAT` variable, and a JSON object envelope with an `advice` array. Agents branch on `result` and `error.code`, not on the exit code.\n\n# Do\n- The `scourge.response.v1` envelope as frozen in M0.7.2.\n- In machine mode every outcome is one JSON object on stdout, including usage and parse errors raised before a workspace is known. Unavailable identifiers are `null`. No prompts, pager, browser or logs on stdout (INV-32).\n- An error says what failed, whether anything was committed, whether an external effect may have occurred, and the next valid step as an argv with its effects.\n- One enumeration of error codes generates the exit-code table (exit 0, 2 to 8). A read that reports a blocker exits 0 with the code in its result.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Machine-mode tests for a parse error, an unknown workspace and a refusal: each is one JSON object.\n- [ ] A test asserts every error code has a row in the generated table, and `--json` equals `--format json`.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.4, §8.5; INV-32.\n\nDiagnostics go to stderr as structured, redacted events keyed by workspace, engagement, request key and run (plan §11.4), never to stdout in machine mode. A test runs a refusal with a seeded secret and checks that stdout is one JSON object and that the stderr events carry the keys and not the secret.\n"}	blake3:Z5qhxr5le7u6zA2-KHYqpyLQj-e_MYxE202WaR6nvE4
1795387075857203	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-r3b	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`show ID [--depth title|summary|full]` works for leads, findings, runs, records and instrument flow IDs. `list [leads|findings|runs]` is the bounded listing the entry screen's omission counts point to.\n\n# Do\n- Implement both as short read transactions.\n- Target content in any output is delimited as untrusted and size-bounded. This is hygiene, not a boundary. Raw evidence is opt-in.\n- A network-sink test over the whole read-only surface: status, help, `show`, `list`, `next`, `--plan`, `doctor` (INV-15).\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] `show` and `list` tests on a seeded store.\n- [ ] The network-sink test fails if any read-only command opens a connection to the target.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §8.3, §8.4; INV-15, INV-22.\n\nImplement the read-only doctor view in this slice. On a seeded store it displays each recorded diagnostic and its age, identifies missing results, and leaves both stores untouched (plan §9.4). Run the network-sink assertion against the commands shipped at this point, and extend it as each later read command lands.\n\nThe network sink also fails on any outbound connection from these commands, not only one to the target. Local reads, status and reports need no telemetry, upload, model provider or Internet access (plan §11.4).\n"}	blake3:9iJ6jHPMhXXG6U0SM8NQpVRCx0SFGWu_OeZtq2vbra0
1795387075935863	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-ijh	{"field":"description","value":"Wire the existing M1 checks into the ordinary project test entry point as they land. For the retained invariants and fixtures in the M0 disposition, identify their named test or qualification runner. Keep host-dependent qualification results separate from the fast local run.\n\nAcceptance\n- On a fresh checkout, the documented command runs the shipped status properties, store tests, read-only network-sink assertions and generated example checks. A deliberately stale example or failing property makes the command fail.\n- Show which retained checks are due in a later milestone and which require a named runner. Skips and unavailable dependencies remain visible in gate results.\n\nSee plan §13–14 and evaluation §13.\n\nAlso in scope:\n- The fuzz corpora of M1.13 replay in the fast run. Longer fuzz runs are recorded separately.\n- The oracle properties of M1.5.7 and the oracle-guide check of M1.5.8 run in the fast run.\n- The instrument contract tests run against the versions pinned in the M0.1 inventory, as a named runner. A change to a pin re-runs them before the new pin is accepted, because Teapot is still moving (plan §15.1, \"Stack drift\").\n"}	blake3:k4sq77pxWNGTE_nSe4EXMgxlsPJ5zu1jgCmUeDaCv5s
1795387076000797	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-lkm	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`web.forbidden-state-change.v1`: the other user or an anonymous caller changes a field of the owner's object. A second oracle on the same observer proves the template interface before anything is generalized. It does not prove an observer interface: the canary observer stays concrete code through M2.\n\n# Do\n- Flows: identity probes; P owner creates the object with canary V0; W2, R2 owner writes V2 then reads (the control, run before the treatment); W1 other user writes V1; R1 owner reads; W3 anonymous write of V3, R3 owner reads; identity probes; cleanup.\n- `true`: R2 shows V2, subjects distinct and unchanged, and R1 shows V1 (`cross_principal`) or R3 shows V3 (`unauthenticated`). `false`: R1 still shows V2 and R3 does not show V3. `invalid`: any reason code, including `OWNER_VIEW_LOST` when R1 shows neither V1 nor V2.\n- The `mutate` effect class: the engagement must allow it.\n- `runtime-intermittent.v1`: a round count declared before the run with no early stop, at least two rounds true, all rounds retained. Display: \"Verified intermittent behavior, k of n rounds\".\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.1)\n- [ ] Fixtures and mutation suite for the state-change oracle, including F-41, a treatment that locks, transfers or deletes the object.\n- [ ] Mixed rounds report `intermittent` and are not verified under the controlled profile.\n\nDepends on M1.5.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.3, §6.6, §7.8; F-41.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n\n# Prerequisites and companions (for the M2.0 split)\n- Development fixture modes: a treatment write that locks, transfers or deletes the order (F-41), and a cross-user write flaw that succeeds in a set fraction of rounds, seeded so a run is reproducible, for the intermittent profile.\n- A guide for `web.forbidden-state-change.v1` in the §6.5 form, checked against the code as in M1.5.8.\n- Property tests over generated observations as in M1.5.7: status codes never change the result, and `OWNER_VIEW_LOST` and every other failure code give `invalid`, never `false`.\n- M1.10.5 waits on this package to screen the write-side Tier 1 families.\n"}	blake3:VyxHLQDc0FY2S6jSo17vZ5zvXfAZplR5hzp-iFMzzkU
1795387076049001	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-1ar	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\n`scourge retest FINDING` compares, it does not merely re-run. A retest is a record like any other and is read by the status function. A fix that breaks login is `inconclusive`, not fixed.\n\n# Do\n- Baseline: the finding's status record, the latest true `verify` record. A retest decides applicability only while its baseline is still the latest true record (INV-51).\n- Modes: `sequential` (default; valid only if the oracle version and experiment digest are unchanged since the baseline) and `paired` (a fresh `true` on the vulnerable build in the same retest).\n- The retest must show: the observer still works and the legitimate path is intact; identities distinct and stable; the treatment no longer shows the violation; the declared variants do not either, with each declared field reported separately; enough rounds were run.\n- Rounds for an intermittent baseline: plan against the one-sided 95% lower bound of the rate, so that the miss probability is at most the engagement's threshold (5% by default). `retest --plan` prints N and its cost in requests first.\n- Two results stored and never merged: `property_result` and `functional_result` (INV-11).\n\n# Acceptance (plan §12.2, row M2.2)\n- [ ] Real fix, broken login, removed endpoint, partial fix, one hidden field and changed oracle each produce the specified pair of results (F-15 to F-19, F-47).\n- [ ] F-46: an intermittent baseline of 2 in 10 retested with 14 rounds is `inconclusive` with a stated miss probability of 59%; with 80 rounds it is `no_violation_within_tested_scope` with 5%.\n- [ ] F-53: a newer true record overtakes a retest that passed. F-52: a retest whose originals are missing is an integrity failure.\n\nDepends on M1.6 and M2.1.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.md §5.1, §6.8; INV-11, INV-51.\n\n# Size\nFiled at work-package level. M2.0 splits it into child bones after the M1.9 trial.\n\n# Prerequisite (for the M2.0 split)\nF-46 needs the fixture's intermittent flaw at a set rate (2 in 10), seeded so that the baseline and both retests are reproducible. It is the fixture mode M2.1 adds.\n"}	blake3:haA2hUL3QWyIDLMOcZZQ_NjP60lKvptrlTvvRliHjD4
1795387076087435	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2vj	{"field":"description","value":"# Context\nThe M2 packages were filed at work-package level from plan revision 5, before any code or measurement existed. Splitting them earlier would have meant guessing.\n\n# Do\nAfter M1.9: go through M2.1 to M2.10, apply what M1 and the cold-agent trial changed, and split remaining large packages into small or medium children with observable acceptance. Keep or revise the existing reporting and durability children. Identify the bone that supplies each retained invariant and fixture check before closing this review.\n\n# Acceptance\n- [ ] Every M2 package is either small enough to do as filed or has child bones.\n- [ ] Packages the evidence no longer supports are closed with a comment that says why.\n\n# Also\n- Record any change to the plan's packages, invariants or fixtures in notes/plan.md as a revision that cites the M1.9 evidence, and keep the README's roadmap in step.\n- M2.11 (the §1.5 run on real binaries) and M2.12 (preview documentation) were added in grooming. Review them with the rest.\n"}	blake3:nqoDY4WFwZLCKJA2Ga4o6iY-VQCV125Pvdapverhy0w
1795387076139881	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2ey	{"field":"description","value":"Implement the recipient command and distinguish the three results in plan §10.2. Use recipient-owned trust settings and locally available oracle versions.\n\nAcceptance\n- Cover a complete bundle, declared omission, missing promised file, altered file and unavailable oracle (F-45). An unsigned copy with a replaced file and matching edited digest has unestablished provenance.\n- A disagreement on re-evaluation fails integrity. Report an obligation as uncheckable when its original is omitted or its evaluator unavailable. Show the historical status at its named snapshot.\n- The command executes no bundled code and sends no traffic.\n\nA bundle comes from someone else, so `bundle check` treats it as hostile input:\n- A manifest path that is absolute, contains `..`, or resolves through a symlink outside the bundle root fails closed, and nothing outside the root is read.\n- An oversized or malformed manifest is rejected with a usage error, never a panic.\n- The manifest reader has a fuzz target whose corpus replays in the fast suite, as in M1.13.\n"}	blake3:-an10aKZKCB5lL365-4mM3y7AKEXxkmbPosKpTzxzm0
1795387076180698	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-qx1	{"field":"description","value":"# Acceptance\n- [ ] A generated command reference.\n- [ ] One guide per oracle in the published form: flows, what each rules out, the predicate, the invalid reasons, the known misses.\n- [ ] A plain statement of what shared custody does not prevent.\n\nRefs: notes/plan.later.md §4; notes/plan.md §6.7.\n\nGenerate the reference from the packaged candidate. Run the documented first-use and offline-check examples on a fresh install. A change to the public command catalog or response schema must surface a stale example in the documentation check.\n\nM2.12 writes the preview documentation. This bone regenerates and checks it for the release candidate.\n"}	blake3:wkPhTaRjFO4Wwri8qVL6R1OJHF8Xdm-g44ynb57mANE
1795387076207354	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2d7	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"owner"}}	blake3:FBro3VDOp8JrGspqW5IB5rnjbq8upYhVVRWS_iP71K4
1795387076231546	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-96y	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"owner"}}	blake3:En3ImTsmRilPog626a7pUUppAHSIGw1teklfiyY2XGE
1795387076254242	gauntlet-dev	itc:AQ		item.update	bn-2hy	{"field":"urgency","value":"punt"}	blake3:zje1b2-XT7Wyz6UgFj2gFn_ykBDW1eWAnCAY8teoWCU
1795389180375479	codex-groom	itc:AQ		item.create	bn-2xz	{"description":"# Context\n\n`notes/plan.later.md` says each later milestone starts with a short design note, written after the preceding milestone, that turns the lower-resolution plan into implementation-ready work. M3 has M3.1 and M5 has M5.0. M4 currently jumps directly to an extra-large target bone; M4.2 is the later oracle design after that target exists, not the milestone re-cut.\n\n# Do\n\nAfter M3 is manually accepted and M3.8 has reported its results:\n\n- Re-read the current Sippycup public interface and schema, and record checkout versus installed identity.\n- Fix the minimum multi-tenant target scope from the M3 evidence and current protocol stack.\n- Split M4.1 and M4.3 into small or medium child bones. Name the owners of target administration and hidden ground truth separately from the implementing agent.\n- Map every fixture in `notes/plan.later.md` section 2.5 to an implementation or integration bone, and give the live experiment, mutation suite, documentation, authority binding, cancellation, and evidence import explicit owners.\n- Update `notes/plan.later.md` if the measured stack or the split changes the milestone contract.\n\n# Acceptance\n\n- [ ] No large M4 implementation placeholder remains dispatchable as one task.\n- [ ] Every section 2.5 fixture and the live M4 gate have a named bone and dependency path.\n- [ ] The target is still built before the media-canary and oracle design; no oracle requirement is invented before the target exists.\n\nRefs: `notes/plan.later.md` introduction and section 2.","kind":"task","labels":["m4","plan"],"parent":"bn-3j7","size":"s","title":"M4.0 Re-cut protocol work from M3 results and the current Sippycup interface","urgency":"punt"}	blake3:GD--w9NGTsVbFSuz1ebUIksPb6VW7yesM-15P6MwKoE
